  "summary_md": "Across staffing and building products, the batch is framed by strategic repositioning and an uneven demand recovery punctuated by sharp event-driven swings. Traditional temporary staffing remains under pressure, with KELYA and JOB reflecting declining volumes, while KFRC breaks from the cyclical trough by pivoting toward higher-margin AI consulting and multi-shore delivery. On the building-products side, the passage of the ROAD to Housing Act handed JELD a policy tailwind and a guidance lift, though near-term fundamentals remain soft. A wave of M&A and transformation stands out: ATLN quickly converted its transformative Circle8 acquisition into large multi-year government framework agreements, APOG moved into higher-growth daylighting solutions with the Kalwall deal, and NIXX captured speculative momentum after posting its first positive operating margin as an AI telecom infrastructure play. Separately, ASPN is navigating a phased production restart following an explosion at its East Providence facility, keeping the recovery timeline at the center of debate. AI and data-center demand—whether for atmospheric water (AIRJ), direct-to-chip cooling (INVE), or district energy (PPIH)—provide a recurring thematic tailwind across both sectors.",
